// BorrowCheck — **E3070: A COLLECTION MAY NOT BE MUTATED WHILE AN ELEMENT BORROWED OUT OF IT IS
// STILL LIVE.**
//
// `arr.get(i)` hands the caller the element POINTER and takes no reference for it
// (`Parser.emitContainerElementAccessor`, `ContainerElementReference.borrowed`) — the array stays the sole owner and its
// `__managed_decref` walk destroys the element. So a write that shortens or replaces the array
// (`clear`, `push` past capacity, a rebind of the binding itself) frees a record the caller is
// still holding: measured **0xC0000005** on `let s = try arr.get(0) otherwise ""` + `arr.clear()`
// + `print("{s}")`, and on five further shapes of the same statement.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THIS IS THE SAME WRITE DOORS THE ITERATION LOCK ALREADY HARDENED (P1.8 slice A), ASKING A
// SECOND QUESTION OF THE SAME BINDING.** The lock asks *"is this binding being iterated?"*; the
// borrow set asks *"is an element of it still referenced?"*. Both are answered from ONE derivation
// of **which storage a write goes through** — `Parser.iterationSubjectNameAt` — so a chain shape
// taught to one is taught to the other, and a write cannot be locked under one key and checked
// under another. That divergence was a segfault once already; see that function's ⚠ block.
//
// ⭐ **WHERE THIS DEPARTS FROM BOTH REFERENCES, AND WHY IT HAD TO.** `BorrowCheckPass.cs` and
// `MaxonBorrowCheck.maxon` are the same algorithm, and both key a borrow on a VAR SLOT recovered
// from `varDecl`/`varLoad`/`varStore` ops. **shv2 has none of those.** `let`/`var` bind a name to
// the initializer's SSA `ValueId` in `Scope` at PARSE TIME (ARCHITECTURE.md, *Names, SSA, and
// phis*), so `let x = 42` mints no op at all: an IR pass cannot see the bindings because the
// bindings are not in the IR. The facts are therefore RECORDED BY THE PARSER, which is the only
// place they exist, and DECIDED here, which is the only place the whole-program parameter-mutation
// summary exists.
//
// Three consequences, each one an improvement on the references rather than a workaround:
//
//   • **Liveness is EXACT, not approximated.** Both references stand in "the highest physical op
//     index this slot appeared at" for the last use. shv2 needs no stand-in: a use of a binding IS
//     a `Scope` resolution (`Parser.requireBindingLive`, the one read chokepoint), and the token
//     cursor is monotone in source order through a function body — the very order the references
//     were approximating.
//   • **`pop`/`remove` are excluded STRUCTURALLY, not by name.** The mint sits at the one door that
//     already decides borrow-vs-move-out (`emitContainerElementAccessor`'s `owned` flag), so v1's
//     hand-maintained `mutatingMethodNames` list (`MaxonBorrowCheck.maxon:42-44`) is not ported —
//     `Parser.arrayMethodMutatesReceiver` is shv2's ONE such list and it already exists.
//   • **NOTHING IS RECORDED UNTIL A FUNCTION ACTUALLY BORROWS.** Every mutation door reads
//     `borrows.count()` first, and a function with no live borrow answers 0 after one read and
//     allocates nothing — its `BorrowFacts` is still the shared empty. v1 rebuilds two arrays per
//     call site (`expireDeadBorrows`, :1099-1114) and the bootstrap allocates a key list on EVERY
//     op (`ExpireBorrows`, :164-171, with no empty-set guard); neither is ported. v1 is debugged,
//     not fast.
//
// ⭐⭐ **A BORROW COMPOSES THROUGH A BORROW, AND NOT THROUGH A CALL — the two halves of "the borrow set
// does not compose", which were ONE SENTENCE until they were measured apart (ARR-borrow).**
//
//   • **THROUGH A BORROW: closed here**, at `composePendingBorrowOntoBases`. An element read out of storage
//     that is itself borrowed is a borrow of that storage's base too, to the root. It is a real
//     use-after-free — measured `0xC0000005` — and the mint is the only place with the binding in hand.
//   • **THROUGH A CALL: deliberately NOT modelled, and it is not a hazard.** `arr.last()` is served from
//     `stdlib/Array.maxon`, whose `returns Element` is a borrowed opaque return, so the CALLEE discharges a
//     real `+1` (`Parser.emitOwnedValueReturn` → `coOwnBorrowedOpaque`) and the caller holds an owned value
//     that cannot dangle. What is missing is a LANGUAGE RULE rather than a reference: `/specs/borrow-checker.md`
//     pins E3070 on that program and the runnable oracle raises it — the bootstrap RETAINS too and refuses
//     anyway. shv2 therefore ACCEPTS `arr.last()` under a live write where the oracle refuses it, today,
//     with no roster strike involved. `Parser.arraySurfaceMemberNames`' first bullet carries that
//     measurement and the design of the column that closes it; it is the half `get`/`first` retire behind.
//
//     ⚖ **THE SCOPE IS THE WHOLE CORPUS-SERVED FAMILY, NOT `arr.last()` — RULED 2026-08-18 (W160), WIDENED
//     AT `W153` BECAUSE THE RETIREMENT IS WHAT MADE THE DIFFERENCE VISIBLE.** This bullet read as a note
//     about one method for as long as `last` was the only accessor a corpus module served. Retiring the
//     synthesized `List` moved `List.first`/`get`/`clear` from compiler-minted to corpus-served in one step,
//     and **five committed cases stopped being refused** (`list/memory.value-survives-clear{,-error,-and-return}`,
//     `borrow-liveness/a-method-rebinding-a-list-field-frees-the-borrowed-element`,
//     `borrow-liveness/a-field-store-in-a-callee-freeing-its-parameters-list`). **All five were measured to
//     compile, run correctly and leak nothing**, so the ruling was to re-pin them here rather than close the
//     gap — which is what those five cases now document.
//
//     ⇒ **The line is WHO MINTS THE BORROW, and it is not about the container.** `arr.get(0)` + `arr.clear()`
//     is STILL refused, because `Parser.dispatchArrayMethod` mints that borrow in the compiler; the same two
//     operations on a `List` are not, because a call is what serves them. **So a container joins this
//     divergence exactly when it is retired into the corpus**, and the next retirement moves its accessors
//     the same way with no edit here — which is the reason this bullet states the family and not a method.
//
// ⚠ **WHAT IS NOT MODELLED, AND IT IS THE REFERENCES' LIMIT TOO: a LOOP makes source order lie.**
// A use textually BEFORE a mutation but inside the same loop body happens after it at run time,
// and a token-ordered last use cannot say so. Both references approximate with a linear op index
// and inherit exactly the same blind spot, so shv2 is no weaker; closing it needs a real liveness
// analysis over the CFG, which is a different mechanism from this one.

// The per-function id of a STORAGE a borrow reads an element of and a write goes through. Dense, and
// in practice tiny — it counts the DISTINCT such storages of one function body — but the range is WIDE
// because nothing CAPS it: a narrow bound here would be a runtime panic on a large machine-generated
// body rather than a diagnostic, and a bound is narrowed only when it is provable.
//
// ⭐ It is minted by resolving a NAME to its BINDING at the instant of the record
// (`Parser.borrowSubjectBindingOf`), so it carries the two properties a name string cannot: a body
// that SHADOWS the source's name declares a different `VarInfo` and gets a different subject
// (correctly writable), and a self-field's two spellings (`items`, `self.items`) reach the ONE alias
// `VarInfo` and so share one subject.
//
// ⚠ **THE SUBJECT SPACE IS WIDER THAN `Scope`.** A top-level `var` is storage a body borrows out of
// and writes exactly as it does a local, and it has no `VarInfo` anywhere — so one is INTERNED per
// name for the file (`VarInfo.createModuleStorageSubject`). Leaving module storage out was a
// reachable use-after-free on four spellings; `specs-shv2/borrow-liveness.md`'s `module-storage-*`
// cases pin each of them.
export typealias BorrowSubjectId = int(0 to u64.max)

// The `via '<x>'` text for a mutation that is a REBIND of the source binding rather than a method
// call on it (`arr = other`, `items = other`, `self.items = other`). Neither reference has one —
// the bootstrap KILLS a borrow at a reassignment (`BorrowCheckPass.cs:129-132`, safe for it because
// it retains the element) — so shv2 names the write with the operator the user wrote.
export let ReassignMutationDisplay = b"="

// ⭐ **AN ELEMENT BORROW THE PARSER HAS MINTED BUT NOT YET BOUND TO A NAME.** A borrow is minted at
// the ACCESSOR (`arr.get(0)`) and completed at the BINDING (`let s = …`), which is where the
// borrowing name and the activation point exist; an accessor whose result no binding takes is never
// completed and is dropped.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT IS KEYED ON THE VALUE THE ACCESSOR PRODUCED, and that is not a detail — it is the only
// key that answers "did the element actually reach this name?".** Two measured defects come from
// keying on POSITION instead:
//
//   • `let n = sizeOf(try arr.get(0) otherwise "")` binds an INTEGER computed from the element. The
//     element reaches no name and nothing can dangle, but a position key hands the borrow to `n` and
//     refuses the program — as it does to a counted range's loop variable, which is the header phi.
//   • `let s = try arr.get(0) otherwise return 1` parses its handler by RE-ENTERING the statement
//     parser mid-initializer, so a position key that resets per statement is wiped there: NO borrow
//     is recorded at all and the following `arr.clear()` is waved through. Measured **0xC0000005**.
//
// A value id is unique within a function, so a stale pending borrow can never be claimed by a later
// binding and no per-statement reset is needed for correctness at all.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE COMPLETION IS WHAT MAKES A MUTATION INSIDE A `try … otherwise` HANDLER LEGAL**, and it
// is the one thing both references are emphatic about (`MbcBorrow.activationIndex`, v1 :425-428;
// `BorrowCheckPass.cs:72-80`). On the handler's path the get FAILED and the borrowing variable was
// never bound, so there is no borrow to conflict with — and since the handler is parsed BEFORE the
// binding completes, its tokens sit below the ACTIVATION and the ordering says so by construction.
// Activating at the CALL instead would refuse `specs/borrow-checker.md:borrow-not-live-in-otherwise`.
export type PendingBorrow
	export let subject as BorrowSubjectId
	// The SSA value the accessor defined. A binding claims this borrow iff the value it binds IS this
	// one — after `Parser.retargetPendingBorrow` has moved it to the merge phi, which is what a
	// `try … otherwise <value>` actually binds.
	export let borrowValue as ValueId
	// The accessor's own line — what the diagnostic reports as `borrowed at line N`.
	export let borrowLine as LineNumber

	export static function create(subject BorrowSubjectId, borrowValue ValueId, borrowLine LineNumber) returns PendingBorrow
		return Self{subject: subject, borrowValue: borrowValue, borrowLine: borrowLine}
	end 'create'
end 'PendingBorrow'

export typealias PendingBorrowArray = Array with PendingBorrow

// A length of, or an index into, the pending-claim list. It is one STATEMENT long
// (`Parser.dropUnclaimedPendingBorrows`) and in practice 0 or 1, but nothing CAPS it — a statement may
// spell as many element accessors as it likes — so the range stays wide until one is provable.
export typealias PendingBorrowCount = int(0 to u64.max)

// A completed borrow: binding `borrower` holds an element of subject `subject` from `activationToken`
// until its own last use.
export type BorrowRecord
	export let subject as BorrowSubjectId
	// ⭐ **THE BORROWING BINDING, HELD BY REFERENCE** — the same discipline
	// `Parser.iterationLockedBindings` states, and here for a second reason: the binding's
	// `lastUseToken` is written by `Parser.requireBindingLive` long AFTER this record is made, so a
	// name copied out at record time would be a second fact free to disagree with the binding it
	// describes. `VarInfo` is a heap `type`, so this record observes every later use.
	export let borrower as VarInfo
	// The token at which the borrowing name came into scope. A write BELOW it — a `try` handler's,
	// or anything earlier in the body — cannot conflict, because the borrow does not exist there yet.
	export let activationToken as TokenIndex
	export let borrowLine as LineNumber

	export static function create(subject BorrowSubjectId, borrower VarInfo, activationToken TokenIndex, borrowLine LineNumber) returns BorrowRecord
		return Self{subject: subject, borrower: borrower, activationToken: activationToken, borrowLine: borrowLine}
	end 'create'
end 'BorrowRecord'

export typealias BorrowRecordArray = Array with BorrowRecord

// A write the parser saw go through a borrow subject. Recorded ONLY while the enclosing function
// already holds at least one completed borrow — a write above every borrow's activation can never
// conflict, so recording one would be work with no question attached.
//
// ⚠ A record `type` rather than the dense scalar columns `CallParamEdges` and `SourceRangeTable`
// insist on, and the gate above is exactly why: those two are built over EVERY call / EVERY op in
// the program, where a heap box per entry is a per-op allocation. This list is empty in every
// function that borrows nothing, which is almost all of them.
export type MutationSite
	export let subject as BorrowSubjectId
	// The name the SOURCE wrote at this write, which is what the diagnostic blames. It may differ
	// from the spelling the borrow used (`items` vs `self.items`) while naming the one subject.
	export let subjectName as ByteArray
	export let token as TokenIndex
	export let line as LineNumber
	export let column as ColumnNumber
	// The `via '<x>'` text: the METHOD name for a container write, the trimmed callee for a free
	// call, `=` for a rebind.
	export let display as ByteArray
	// ⭐ **NON-EMPTY ⇒ THIS SITE IS ONLY A WRITE IF THE CALLEE SAYS SO.** A container method and a
	// rebind are decided by the parser, which holds the whole question; `grow(arr)` is not — whether
	// it writes depends on `grow`'s BODY, possibly in another file. Those sites carry the callee and
	// the argument's column, and `mutationSiteWrites` settles them against the whole-program
	// `ParamMutationSummary` — the SAME summary E3019's call-site half reads, never a second one.
	export let callee as ByteArray
	// The argument's index in the call op's OWN columns (a prepended method receiver included), which
	// is the index `argSlotPosition` maps to a parameter position. Meaningless when `callee` is empty.
	export let argColumn as ParamCount
	export let argLabel as ByteArray

	export static function createContainerWrite(subject BorrowSubjectId, subjectName ByteArray, token TokenIndex, line LineNumber, column ColumnNumber, display ByteArray) returns MutationSite
		return Self{subject: subject, subjectName: subjectName, token: token, line: line, column: column, display: display, callee: EmptySourceText, argColumn: 0, argLabel: EmptySourceText}
	end 'createContainerWrite'

	export static function createCallArgWrite(subject BorrowSubjectId, subjectName ByteArray, token TokenIndex, line LineNumber, column ColumnNumber, callee ByteArray, argColumn ParamCount, argLabel ByteArray) returns MutationSite
		return Self{subject: subject, subjectName: subjectName, token: token, line: line, column: column, display: calleeDisplayName(callee), callee: callee, argColumn: argColumn, argLabel: argLabel}
	end 'createCallArgWrite'
end 'MutationSite'

export typealias MutationSiteArray = Array with MutationSite

// ⭐⭐⭐ **"THE CALLER'S PARAMETER `p` OWNS THE ARRAY THIS CALL'S RECEIVER DENOTES" — the SECOND edge kind
// the storage column is closed over, and the one without which a retired member's write stops reaching
// the caller (ARR4).**
//
// `SemanticCheck.collectCallParamEdges` records an edge only for an argument that IS one of the caller's
// own parameters, because that is the whole of what E3019's column needs. E3070's column needs more: a
// write reaches a caller through any array the caller's parameter OWNS, and `self.items` is such an array
// while being a FIELD LOAD rather than a parameter value. While every array mutator was a roster arm the
// gap was invisible — `Parser.noteBorrowSubjectWrite` stamps the enclosing method's bit directly, off a
// `receiverStorage` mask the parser had already derived. A member served from `stdlib/Array.maxon` reaches
// no such door, and nothing carried the fact.
//
// ⛔ **MEASURED — a use-after-free with the suite green over it.** `type Bag`'s `wipe()` whose body is
// `items.truncate(0)`, called while the caller holds `let s = try b.items.get(0) otherwise ""`, compiled
// clean and printed **4557430888798830399** (`__mm_free`'s `0x3F` poison read back as a byte length) where
// the oracle prints 44. `truncate` has never been on `Parser.arraySurfaceMemberNames`, so this was live on
// `main` with no roster strike at all; `insert` (ARR1) and `reserve` (ARR2) opened it and `clear` (ARR4)
// would have widened it. `borrow-liveness.receiver-method-writing-its-own-field-through-a-corpus-member`
// and `.a-free-callee-writing-a-field-of-its-parameter` are the two programs, seen RED before the edge.
//
// ⚠ **STORAGE-ONLY, AND THAT IS THE RULING RATHER THAN A CONSERVATISM.** Feeding this edge into
// `writtenParamMask` would make a caller's `let b` an E3019 for a method writing its own field — the
// answer `specs-shv2/self-keyword.md:self-with-params` and
// `parameter-mutation:let-struct-with-array-field-to-mutating-method-ok` both pin the other way. See
// `IrFunction`'s two-mask table, whose first row is exactly this write.
//
// ⚠ **IT SAYS NOTHING ABOUT WHETHER THE CALLEE WRITES** — that is the fixpoint's answer, arrived at
// through this edge, and asking it here would need a summary that does not exist until every file is
// parsed. `borrow-liveness.a-corpus-served-read-only-member-is-not-a-conflict` is the over-rejection
// guard: `items.capacity()` records an edge and no bit ever travels it.
export type StorageReachEdge
	// The callee this receiver was handed to, as the call op spells it.
	export let callee as ByteArray
	// The argument column the RECEIVER fills in the call op's own columns — the index `argSlotPosition`
	// maps to a parameter POSITION of the callee, exactly as `MutationSite.argColumn` is.
	export let argColumn as ParamCount
	// Which of the CALLER's parameters own the storage that receiver denotes (`Parser.subjectStorageMask`).
	export let callerParams as ParamPositionMask

	export static function create(callee ByteArray, argColumn ParamCount, callerParams ParamPositionMask) returns StorageReachEdge
		return Self{callee: callee, argColumn: argColumn, callerParams: callerParams}
	end 'create'
end 'StorageReachEdge'

export typealias StorageReachEdgeArray = Array with StorageReachEdge

// ⭐ **THE PER-FUNCTION BORROW FACTS — written by the PARSER, read HERE.** They ride on `IrFunction`
// for the reason `writtenParamMask` does: the parser holds the only view in which a binding exists,
// and the merged module is the only place the whole program does.
//
// ⚠ **A FUNCTION THAT BORROWS NOTHING SHARES ONE OBJECT** (`sharedEmptyBorrowFacts`) and allocates
// nothing at all, so every mutation door's `borrows.count() == 0` gate is one read against a
// permanently empty array. It is COPY-ON-WRITE (`Parser.borrowFactsOf`) and the shared instance is
// NEVER mutated — the same invariant `sharedEmptyIterationLocks` carries, and it must hold for the
// same reason: the per-file parse fan-out shares it across threads.
export type BorrowFacts
	// The distinct storages this body borrows from or writes, in first-seen order — the id space
	// `BorrowSubjectId` indexes. Parser working state; the resolver reads only its COUNT, as the key
	// space the borrow buckets are built over.
	export var subjects as VarInfoArray
	export var pending as PendingBorrowArray
	export var borrows as BorrowRecordArray
	export var mutations as MutationSiteArray
	// ⚠ **THE ONE COLUMN HERE THAT IS NOT ABOUT THIS BODY'S OWN BORROWS** — see `StorageReachEdge`. It
	// rides on the same record for the same reason the rest do (the parser holds the only view in which a
	// binding exists) and, unlike them, it is recorded whether or not this body borrows anything: the
	// borrow it protects belongs to a CALLER. It is empty for every function that calls no array member on
	// storage a parameter owns, which is nearly all of them, so the shared-empty gate still pays for itself.
	export var storageReach as StorageReachEdgeArray

	export static function create() returns BorrowFacts
		return Self{subjects: VarInfoArray.create(), pending: PendingBorrowArray.create(), borrows: BorrowRecordArray.create(), mutations: MutationSiteArray.create(), storageReach: StorageReachEdgeArray.create()}
	end 'create'
end 'BorrowFacts'

// The one instance every function starts at — see `BorrowFacts`'s ⚠ block for the invariant.
export let sharedEmptyBorrowFacts = BorrowFacts.create()

// The `via '<x>'` text for a FREE call: the callee's UNQUALIFIED name, so `Bag.wipe` reads `wipe` and
// a bare `grow` is unchanged. v1's `formatCalleeDisplay` (`MaxonBorrowCheck.maxon:1059-1076`) trims
// the same way.
//
// ⚠ It goes through `splitQualifiedName`, the tree's ONE home for "where does a qualified name
// divide". A hand-rolled scan here would be a FOURTH spelling of the separator, beside
// `QualifiedAliasSeparatorByte`, `containsQualifier` and `Parser.MethodNameSeparatorBytes` — and a
// divergence between them would be a wrong string in a diagnostic, not a compile error.
//
// ⚠ The bootstrap trims after the FIRST dot instead (`FormatMethodName`, :332-335), which on its own
// namespaced names yields `StringArray.push` — a divergence with no corpus case either way, because
// the only qualified callee that can mutate a borrow subject is a static taking the array as an
// ordinary argument. shv2 follows v1: the unqualified name is what the user reads at the call.
function calleeDisplayName(callee ByteArray) returns ByteArray
	return match splitQualifiedName(callee) 'display'
		parts(_, member) gives member
		notQualified gives callee
	end 'display'
end 'calleeDisplayName'

// The E3070 wire text, in ONE place. Byte-identical to both reference compilers' — the four
// `maxoncstderr` goldens this rung enables pin every word of it.
function borrowConflictMessage(subjectName ByteArray, display ByteArray, borrowerName ByteArray, borrowLine LineNumber) returns String
	return "cannot mutate '{String.from(subjectName)}' via '{String.from(display)}' while it is borrowed by '{String.from(borrowerName)}' (borrowed at line {borrowLine})"
end 'borrowConflictMessage'

// ⭐ **THE ONE RESOLVER.** Every E3070 in the program comes out of `reportFirstLiveBorrow` below, so
// there is exactly one message format and one anchor rule however the write was spelled.
//
// Run from `semanticCheck`, and it must be: the free-call half needs the whole-program
// `ParamMutationSummary`, which does not exist until every file is parsed. It adds no `CompilePhase`
// for that reason too — the timing tables and `docs/optimization-log.md` stay comparable.
export function checkBorrowConflicts(project Project, module MaxonModule, mutationSummary ParamMutationSummary)
	for func in module.functions 'eachFunc'
		checkFunctionBorrowConflicts(project, func: func, mutationSummary: mutationSummary)
	end 'eachFunc'
end 'checkBorrowConflicts'

function checkFunctionBorrowConflicts(project Project, func IrFunction, mutationSummary ParamMutationSummary)
	let facts = func.borrowFacts
	if facts.borrows.count() == 0 'borrowsNothing'
		return
	end 'borrowsNothing'

	// ⭐ **THE BORROWS, BUCKETED BY SUBJECT — built ONCE per function, never per site.** A site then
	// walks only the borrows of the storage it writes, instead of every borrow in the body. The
	// bucket order is the borrows' own append order, which is activation order, which is what makes
	// "blame the FIRST active borrow" (v1 :1118-1123) a property of the program rather than of a
	// map's iteration: `multiple-refs-survive-array-growth` blames `'a'`, not `'c'`.
	//
	// Both references instead REBUILD their active set at every op — the bootstrap allocates a key
	// list per op (`ExpireBorrows`, :164-171) and v1 two arrays per call site (`expireDeadBorrows`,
	// :1099-1114). Nothing is allocated per site here.
	var subjectKeys = CsrColumn.create()
	var borrowRows = CsrColumn.create()
	var previousActivation = 0
	for i in 0 upto facts.borrows.count() 'eachBorrow'
		let borrow = borrowAt(facts, index: i)
		// ⚠ **THE ORDER THE WALK BELOW IS AMORTIZED ON, CHECKED WHERE IT IS ESTABLISHED.** See
		// `reportFirstLiveBorrow`: it stops at the first not-yet-active borrow instead of walking past it,
		// which is only equivalent because activation is non-decreasing. It is, because every activation is
		// `Parser.attachPendingBorrows`'s `self.pos` and the cursor is monotone through a function body — the
		// same invariant `Parser.requireBindingLive`'s forward-only rule rests on. A parser change that
		// rewound the cursor mid-body would silently STOP REPORTING conflicts, which is a use-after-free
		// nobody would be told about, so it says so here instead.
		if borrow.activationToken < previousActivation 'borrowsOutOfActivationOrder'
			panic("checkFunctionBorrowConflicts: borrow {i} activates at token {borrow.activationToken}, below its predecessor's {previousActivation} — the parse cursor rewound inside a function body and the live-borrow walk's ordering no longer holds")
		end 'borrowsOutOfActivationOrder'
		previousActivation = borrow.activationToken

		subjectKeys.push(borrow.subject)
		borrowRows.push(i)
	end 'eachBorrow'
	let bySubject = buildCsr(subjectKeys, to: borrowRows, nodeCount: facts.subjects.count())

	// ⭐⭐ **THE PER-SUBJECT WALK CURSOR — what makes this pass LINEAR instead of sites × borrows.** A
	// borrow that is DEAD at one site is dead at every LATER site (its last use is fixed once parsing ends,
	// and the sites are visited in token order), so the walk below never has to look at it again: the cursor
	// only ever moves FORWARD, and across the whole function it advances at most once per borrow. The pass
	// is therefore O(borrows + sites) rather than O(sites × borrows-of-that-subject).
	//
	// MEASURED, on `Testing/ladders/genborrow.sh <K> dead` — K dead borrows and K writes of one array in one
	// body, which is the shape both references also walk in full. Before: 0.40M / 1.53M / 5.85M / 23.7M /
	// 94.3M CPU ticks over K = 64 … 1024, a clean ×3.8 ×3.8 ×4.1 ×4.0 per doubling — a quadratic.
	// One entry per subject, so a body that borrows one array pays one word.
	var walked = CsrColumn.create()
	walked.resize(facts.subjects.count())

	var previousSiteToken = 0
	for s in 0 upto facts.mutations.count() 'eachSite'
		let site = try facts.mutations.get(s) otherwise panic("checkFunctionBorrowConflicts: mutations.get OOB at {s}")
		// The other half of the same invariant, and it carries the same consequence — see the borrow loop.
		//
		// ⚠ **BUT NOT THE SAME CAUSE, AND IT STOPPED BEING THE SAME AT W61.** A borrow's activation is the
		// parse cursor, so a decrease there means the cursor rewound. A SITE's token is the closing `)` of
		// its argument list (`Parser.containerWriteToken`), which is NOT the order the sites are recorded in:
		// a container write nested inside another's arguments is recorded second and happens FIRST. So this
		// order is a POSTCONDITION of `Parser.pushMutationSiteInTokenOrder`, which restores it at the one
		// push, and a decrease here means that recorder is broken rather than the cursor.
		// MEASURED, with the ordered insert removed: `xs.set(1, value: try ys.pop() otherwise "z")` under a
		// live borrow of `xs` panicked here — site 1 at token 55 below its predecessor's 58.
		if site.token < previousSiteToken 'sitesOutOfTokenOrder'
			panic("checkFunctionBorrowConflicts: mutation site {s} is at token {site.token}, below its predecessor's {previousSiteToken} — `Parser.pushMutationSiteInTokenOrder` did not keep the sites non-decreasing, and the live-borrow walk's cursor no longer holds")
		end 'sitesOutOfTokenOrder'
		previousSiteToken = site.token

		if not mutationSiteWrites(project, site: site, mutationSummary: mutationSummary) 'notAWriteAfterAll'
			continue
		end 'notAWriteAfterAll'

		reportFirstLiveBorrow(project, func: func, facts: facts, bySubject: bySubject, walked: walked, site: site)
	end 'eachSite'
end 'checkFunctionBorrowConflicts'

// Does this recorded site actually WRITE its subject? A container method and a rebind were settled
// by the parser, which held the whole question. A call ARGUMENT was not: it writes iff the callee
// writes the parameter it fills, which is what the whole-program summary answers — and it is
// answered through the SAME `argSlotPosition` bridge `checkImmutableArgToMutatingParam` uses, so
// the source-order column and the declaration-order mask are never bridged twice.
function mutationSiteWrites(project Project, site MutationSite, mutationSummary ParamMutationSummary) returns bool
	if site.callee.isEmpty() 'settledAtTheParse'
		return true
	end 'settledAtTheParse'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE STORAGE COLUMN, NOT E3019's** — the two ask different questions and shv2 answers them
	// differently (`IrFunction`'s table). `Bag.wipe`'s `maskOf` is 0 by the ruling
	// `self-keyword.md:self-with-params` pins, and reading it here is what let `b.wipe()` free a borrowed
	// element in silence. Its `storageMaskOf` carries the receiver's bit, so the same call is refused.
	//
	// It is the storage column for EVERY call site, argument and receiver alike: E3070's question is always
	// "does the callee write the array I handed it?", and that is the one this column answers.
	//
	// ⚠ **NOT because the storage mask contains the other one — it does not** (`IrFunction`'s table lists
	// four divergences in both directions). `grow(arr)` is unaffected for a concrete reason instead: the
	// ARRAY dispatcher records BOTH masks for a parameter receiver, so a plain mutating callee carries its
	// bit in each. Do not restate this as containment; the `String`/`Set` rows go the other way.
	let mask = mutationSummary.storageMaskOf(site.callee)
	if mask == NoParamPositions 'writesNoParameter'
		return false
	end 'writesNoParameter'

	// An undeclared callee is E3004 from `validateCall`; there is no signature to slot against and no
	// second diagnostic to add here.
	let sig = try project.funcSignatures.get(site.callee) otherwise return false
	let pos = argSlotPosition(sig.paramNames, argIndex: site.argColumn, label: site.argLabel)
	return pos < sig.paramCount() and maskHasParam(mask, param: pos)
end 'mutationSiteWrites'

// Report the FIRST borrow of this site's subject that is already active and not yet dead, and stop —
// one diagnostic per write, naming one borrow, exactly as both references do. Anchored on the WRITE,
// which is the line:col the four goldens pin.
//
// ⭐ `walked` is the per-subject cursor described at its declaration: the count of this subject's
// borrows already proved DEAD, which no later site need look at again. It is advanced here and read
// nowhere else.
function reportFirstLiveBorrow(project Project, func IrFunction, facts BorrowFacts, bySubject CsrGraph, walked CsrColumn, site MutationSite)
	let degree = bySubject.degree(site.subject)
	var k = try walked.get(site.subject) otherwise panic("reportFirstLiveBorrow: walked.get OOB at subject {site.subject}, a key the CSR was built over")
	while k < degree 'eachBorrowOfThisSubject'
		let borrow = borrowAt(facts, index: bySubject.neighbour(site.subject, k: k))

		// NOT YET BOUND at this write (a `try` handler's, or anything earlier in the body): the
		// borrowing name does not exist there, so there is nothing to conflict with — and neither is any
		// borrow BEHIND it, because activation is non-decreasing along a bucket (checked where the buckets
		// are built). The cursor is NOT advanced: this borrow becomes live at a later site.
		if borrow.activationToken > site.token 'notBorrowedYet'
			return
		end 'notBorrowedYet'

		// NON-LEXICAL LIFETIMES: the borrow ends at the borrowing binding's LAST USE, not at its
		// scope's end. `lastUseToken` is seeded to the activation, so a binding never read again is
		// dead the instant it is bound — which is what makes `borrow-nll-allowed` compile.
		//
		// ⭐ **AND IT IS DEAD FOR EVERY LATER SITE TOO**, since `lastUseToken` is final once parsing ends and
		// the sites arrive in token order — so the cursor steps over it once and for all. That is the whole
		// of what makes this pass linear.
		if borrow.borrower.lastUseToken <= site.token 'borrowAlreadyDead'
			k = k + 1
			try walked.set(site.subject, value: k) otherwise panic("reportFirstLiveBorrow: walked.set OOB at subject {site.subject}, the index just read")
			continue
		end 'borrowAlreadyDead'

		reportPositioned(project, code: ErrorCode.semanticBorrowConflict, message: borrowConflictMessage(site.subjectName, display: site.display, borrowerName: borrow.borrower.name, borrowLine: borrow.borrowLine), line: site.line, column: site.column, filePath: func.sourceFilePath)
		return
	end 'eachBorrowOfThisSubject'
end 'reportFirstLiveBorrow'

// The one indexed read of the borrow column, so the two walks above share its bound message.
function borrowAt(facts BorrowFacts, index CsrIndex) returns BorrowRecord
	return try facts.borrows.get(index) otherwise panic("BorrowCheck: borrows.get OOB at {index} — the subject buckets are built over this very column")
end 'borrowAt'

// The one indexed read of the pending-claim column — `borrowAt`'s twin, and for its reason: four walks
// index this list (`Parser.retargetPendingBorrow`, `.attachPendingBorrows`, and the two halves of the
// composition rule below) and each had spelled its own bound message.
export function pendingAt(facts BorrowFacts, index PendingBorrowCount) returns PendingBorrow
	return try facts.pending.get(index) otherwise panic("BorrowCheck: pending.get OOB at {index} — every walk of this list is bounded by its own count()")
end 'pendingAt'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE COMPOSITION RULE: A BORROW OF STORAGE THAT IS ITSELF BORROWED IS A BORROW OF ITS BASE TOO,
// UP TO THE ROOT.** Given the entries `Parser.mintPendingBorrow` has just pushed from `firstPending` on,
// push one more for every storage those subjects are themselves borrowed out of.
//
// ⛔ **MEASURED 0xC0000005 WITHOUT IT, on a program `dispatchArrayMethod`'s `createCursor` arm predicted in
// as many words and left to this rung**: `let c = try xs.managed.createCursor()`, `let s = c.current()`,
// `xs.clear()`, `print(s)`. `c`'s borrow of `xs` is NLL and dies at `c`'s last use — line 4, above the
// write — while `s`, keyed on `c` alone, names an element still living inside `xs`. Neither borrow refused
// the write and the element was freed under `s`.
// `borrow-liveness.error.a-borrow-taken-out-of-a-borrow-holds-the-root` is that program, seen RED first.
//
// ⭐ **IT IS THE ONE HOME OF THE RULE, AND IT IS THE MINT AND NOT THE RESOLVER.** Composing here means a
// composed borrow is an ORDINARY `BorrowRecord` from the instant it exists — same bucket, same activation
// order, same NLL expiry, same diagnostic — so `reportFirstLiveBorrow` needs no second notion of a borrow
// and `a-composed-borrow-expires-at-its-own-last-use` (the over-rejection guard) holds by construction
// rather than by a second rule agreeing with the first. v1 composes at the other end, as a per-VALUE
// `baseOf` chain re-derived per function in the Std IR (`StdLiveness.maxon:236-267`); shv2 has the binding
// in hand at the mint and no liveness pass to re-derive it in, so the chain is walked once, where it is
// created, over a relation the parser already holds.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS A WALK OVER PENDINGS AND NOT A `baseOf` CHAIN, because a binding may borrow MORE THAN ONE
// STORAGE** — `let s = match m 'p' first gives try a.get(0) … second gives try b.get(0) … end 'p'` leaves
// `s` borrowing both (`attachPendingBorrows`'s ⚠ block, which is why that walk runs past its first match).
// A single-successor `baseOf` would silently drop one of the two arms, waving through a write to it.
//
// ⚠ **A FUNCTION THAT HOLDS NO BORROW PAYS ONE COMPARISON.** `facts.borrows` is empty in almost every body,
// so the inner walk does nothing and the outer loop runs exactly once over the entry just pushed — the same
// reading `functionHoldsABorrow`'s gate states, at the one door that cannot be gated on it (a mint happens
// precisely when no borrow exists yet).
export function composePendingBorrowOntoBases(facts BorrowFacts, firstPending PendingBorrowCount, borrowValue ValueId, range SourceRange)
	var scan = firstPending
	while scan < facts.pending.count() 'eachLinkOfTheChain'
		let borrowedStorage = try facts.subjects.get(pendingAt(facts, index: scan).subject) otherwise panic("composePendingBorrowOntoBases: subjects.get OOB at the subject of pending {scan} — a subject id is minted by `Parser.borrowSubjectIdOf` against this very column")
		for k in 0 upto facts.borrows.count() 'eachBorrowOfThisBody'
			let base = borrowAt(facts, index: k)

			// Object identity, the key `borrowSubjectIdOf` mints its ids under: this is "is the storage that
			// element came out of the SAME BINDING some earlier borrow named as its borrower?".
			if not (base.borrower is borrowedStorage) 'someOtherBorrower'
				continue
			end 'someOtherBorrower'

			// Already on this chain. The test is what makes the walk terminate at all — a `var` rebound to
			// something borrowed out of its own borrower is a CYCLE in this relation, and without it the loop
			// would push for ever. It also keeps one base from being pushed twice when two links share it.
			if pendingCarriesSubject(facts, from: firstPending, subject: base.subject) 'alreadyOnTheChain'
				continue
			end 'alreadyOnTheChain'

			// The BORROW LINE stays the reading's own — the element the user is about to hold was read HERE,
			// and `borrowed at line N` must send them to the line they wrote, never to the base's own mint.
			facts.pending.push(PendingBorrow.create(base.subject, borrowValue: borrowValue, borrowLine: range.line))
		end 'eachBorrowOfThisBody'
		scan = scan + 1
	end 'eachLinkOfTheChain'
end 'composePendingBorrowOntoBases'

// Does the chain built from `from` on already name `subject`? See the ⚠ block above — this is the
// termination condition, not a tidiness check.
function pendingCarriesSubject(facts BorrowFacts, from PendingBorrowCount, subject BorrowSubjectId) returns bool
	for k in from upto facts.pending.count() 'eachLinkSoFar'
		if pendingAt(facts, index: k).subject == subject 'alreadyNamed'
			return true
		end 'alreadyNamed'
	end 'eachLinkSoFar'
	return false
end 'pendingCarriesSubject'
